<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex161.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex161.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q_{M}\beta\sum_{k=0}^{t-1}\beta^{k}(1+q_{M})^{k}\|\mathbf{Z}_{t-k-1}\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q_{M}\beta\sum_{k=0}^{t-1}\beta^{k}(1+q_{M})^{k}\|\mathbf{Z}_{t-k-1}\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex162"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex162.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex162.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{q_{M}\beta}{1-\beta(1+q_{M})}\bigg(\frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{B}}+\sqrt{\frac{1-\beta}{(1+\beta)}}\cdot\frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{B}}+G+q_{G}(\sigma+G)+\bigg." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>σ</mi><msqrt><mi>B</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>σ</mi><msqrt><mi>B</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">+</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{q_{M}\beta}{1-\beta(1+q_{M})}\bigg(\frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{B}}+\sqrt{\frac{1-\beta}{(1+\beta)}}\cdot\frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{B}}+G+q_{G}(\sigma+G)+\bigg.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bigg.q_{W}(1+q_{G})DL+(1+q_{W})(1+q_{G})t\eta\sqrt{r}L\bigg)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>W</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>r</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bigg.q_{W}(1+q_{G})DL+(1+q_{W})(1+q_{G})t\eta\sqrt{r}L\bigg).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS9.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS9.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">The second inequality is due to the triangle inequality. The third inequality is due to Definition <a href="#A2.Ex1" title="In B.1 Preliminaries ‣ Appendix B Proof of Theorem ‣ A Convergence Analysis of Adaptive Optimizers under Floating-point Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.6</span></a>. The fourth inequality is due to the triangle inequality. The fifth inequality is due to <math id="A2.SS9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}_{0}=\mathbf{M}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}_{0}=\mathbf{M}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. The last inequality we used Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma B.6. ‣ B.8 Proof of Lemma ‣ Appendix B Proof of Theorem ‣ A Convergence Analysis of Adaptive Optimizers under Floating-point Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.6</span></a>
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Additional Experiments and Details</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Imitating Quantization and Dequantization</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We emulate floating-point quantization and dequantization by reducing the mantissa length from its original precision (52 bits for <span id="A3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">float64</span> and 23 bits for <span id="A3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">float32</span>) to <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> bits, while keeping the exponent and sign bits unchanged.
This design choice is motivated by the fact that practical scaling techniques can effectively prevent overflow and underflow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2023</a>]</cite>.
After truncating the mantissa, we apply stochastic rounding to the nearest two representable values, and then dequantize the result back to standard <span id="A3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">float32</span> or <span id="A3.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">float64</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Synthetic Experiments</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct synthetic experiments on the Rosenbrock function, defined as</p>
<table id="A3.EGx189" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle F(\mathbf{W})=\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}\Big(100\|\mathbf{W}_{j+1}-\mathbf{W}_{j}^{2}\|_{F}^{2}+\|\mathbf{1}_{m}-\mathbf{W}_{j}\|_{F}^{2}\Big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle F(\mathbf{W})=\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}\Big(100\|\mathbf{W}_{j+1}-\mathbf{W}_{j}^{2}\|_{F}^{2}+\|\mathbf{1}_{m}-\mathbf{W}_{j}\|_{F}^{2}\Big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}=[\mathbf{W}_{1},\mathbf{W}_{2},\dots,\mathbf{W}_{d}]\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}=[\mathbf{W}_{1},\mathbf{W}_{2},\dots,\mathbf{W}_{d}]\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the weight matrix.
The global minimum is at <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{*}=[\mathbf{1}_{m},\mathbf{1}_{m},\dots,\mathbf{1}_{m}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{*}=[\mathbf{1}_{m},\mathbf{1}_{m},\dots,\mathbf{1}_{m}]</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\mathbf{W}^{*})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\mathbf{W}^{*})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
We set <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=50</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=100</annotation></semantics></math>, and initialize <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{1}_{m\times n},0.1^{2}\mathbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mn>0.1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{1}_{m\times n},0.1^{2}\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math>.
For Muon, we apply the default hyperparameters in the Newton-Schulz iteration to compute the zeroth power / orthogonalization of <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jordan et al., 2024</a>]</cite>, using double precision.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.1 Theoretical results of Adam ‣ 4 Main Results ‣ A Convergence Analysis of Adaptive Optimizers under Floating-point Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the gradient norms of Adam with different quantization errors on the Rosenbrock function. Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.2 Theoretical results of Muon ‣ 4 Main Results ‣ A Convergence Analysis of Adaptive Optimizers under Floating-point Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows the gradient norms of Muon with different quantization errors on the Rosenbrock function.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ C.2 Synthetic Experiments ‣ Appendix C Additional Experiments and Details ‣ A Convergence Analysis of Adaptive Optimizers under Floating-point Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows the function values of Adam with different quantization errors on the Rosenbrock function. Figure <a href="#A3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ C.2 Synthetic Experiments ‣ Appendix C Additional Experiments and Details ‣ A Convergence Analysis of Adaptive Optimizers under Floating-point Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows the function values of Muon with different quantization errors on the Rosenbrock function. The relative quantization error is defined as <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\|\mathbf{X}-Q(\mathbf{X})\|_{F}}{\|\mathbf{X}\|_{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\|\mathbf{X}-Q(\mathbf{X})\|_{F}}{\|\mathbf{X}\|_{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, measuring the average quantization error of <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the quantization operator.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.21314v1/adam_qe_rosenbrock.svg" id="A3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:467/332;" width="467" height="332"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A3.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="A3.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Rosenbrock: Adam relative quantization error of different mantissa bits (<math id="A3.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>). Weights error (top left), Gradient error (top right), First moment error (bottom left), Second moment error (bottom right). These results show that the more mantissa bits, the smaller the relative quantization error. Combining with Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.1 Theoretical results of Adam ‣ 4 Main Results ‣ A Convergence Analysis of Adaptive Optimizers under Floating-point Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we can see that the more mantissa bits, the smaller quantization error, the better convergence performance (Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 4.5 (Convergence of Quantized Adam). ‣ 4.1 Theoretical results of Adam ‣ 4 Main Results ‣ A Convergence Analysis of Adaptive Optimizers under Floating-point Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A3.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.21314v1/muon_qe_rosenbrock.svg" id="A3.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:467/107;" width="467" height="107"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A3.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="A3.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Rosenbrock: Muon relative quantization error of different mantissa bits (<math id="A3.F6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>). Weights error (left), Gradient error (middle), Momentum error (right). These results show that the more mantissa bits, the smaller the relative quantization error. Combining with Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.2 Theoretical results of Muon ‣ 4 Main Results ‣ A Convergence Analysis of Adaptive Optimizers under Floating-point Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we can see that the more mantissa bits, the smaller quantization error, the better convergence performance (Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 4.6 (Convergence of Quantized Muon). ‣ 4.2 Theoretical results of Muon ‣ 4 Main Results ‣ A Convergence Analysis of Adaptive Optimizers under Floating-point Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>Real-data Experiments</h3>

<div id="A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct real-data experiments on the CIFAR-10 dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krizhevsky et al., 2009</a>]</cite> using a 4-layer fully connected network (FCN).
The architecture is as follows: an input layer with 3072 neurons (corresponding to <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\times 32\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\times 32\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> images), followed by three hidden layers with 512, 256, and 64 neurons, respectively, and an output layer with 10 neurons for classification.
ReLU activations are used for all hidden layers, and the network is trained with the cross-entropy loss for 100 epochs.
We evaluate both Adam and Muon under varying quantization precisions.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For Adam, we use mantissa bit-lengths <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\{1,2,3,7,10,23\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>23</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\{1,2,3,7,10,23\}</annotation></semantics></math>, batch size <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=256</annotation></semantics></math>, learning rate <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=1.5\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=1.5\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}=0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}=0.95</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}=0.999" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.999</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}=0.999</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=10^{-8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=10^{-8}</annotation></semantics></math>, and weight decay <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math>.
For Muon, vector parameters are updated using Adam, while matrix parameters are updated with Muon’s orthogonalization step.
We choose mantissa bit-lengths <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\{2,3,7,10,23\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>23</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\{2,3,7,10,23\}</annotation></semantics></math>, batch size <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=512</annotation></semantics></math>, learning rate <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=0.001</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.99" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.99</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.99</annotation></semantics></math>, weight decay <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, and 5 Newton–Schulz iterations, following the iteration hyperparameters in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jordan et al. [2024]</a></cite>. The auxiliary Adam optimizer in Muon uses learning rate <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=2\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=2\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}=0.9</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}=0.999" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.999</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}=0.999</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=10^{-8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=10^{-8}</annotation></semantics></math>, and weight decay <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.05</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.05</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ C.3 Real-data Experiments ‣ Appendix C Additional Experiments and Details ‣ A Convergence Analysis of Adaptive Optimizers under Floating-point Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows the gradient norms of Adam with different quantization errors on CIFAR-10. Figure <a href="#A3.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ C.3 Real-data Experiments ‣ Appendix C Additional Experiments and Details ‣ A Convergence Analysis of Adaptive Optimizers under Floating-point Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> shows the gradient norms of Muon with different quantization errors on CIFAR-10.
Figure <a href="#A3.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ C.3 Real-data Experiments ‣ Appendix C Additional Experiments and Details ‣ A Convergence Analysis of Adaptive Optimizers under Floating-point Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> shows the quantization errors of Adam with different precision on CIFAR-10. Figure <a href="#A3.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ C.3 Real-data Experiments ‣ Appendix C Additional Experiments and Details ‣ A Convergence Analysis of Adaptive Optimizers under Floating-point Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> shows the quantization errors of Muon with different precision on CIFAR-10. The relative quantization error is defined as <math id="A3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\|\mathbf{X}-Q(\mathbf{X})\|_{F}}{\|\mathbf{X}\|_{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\|\mathbf{X}-Q(\mathbf{X})\|_{F}}{\|\mathbf{X}\|_{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, measuring the average quantization error of <math id="A3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the quantization operator.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.21314v1/adam_cifar10_grad.svg" id="A3.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:467/304;" width="467" height="304"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A3.F7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="A3.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">CIFAR-10: Adam gradient norms under different mantissa precisions <math id="A3.F7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Larger mantissa bit-lengths lead to smaller converged gradient norms. Together with Figure <a href="#A3.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ C.3 Real-data Experiments ‣ Appendix C Additional Experiments and Details ‣ A Convergence Analysis of Adaptive Optimizers under Floating-point Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, this demonstrates that higher precision reduces quantization error and improves convergence, consistent with Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 4.5 (Convergence of Quantized Adam). ‣ 4.1 Theoretical results of Adam ‣ 4 Main Results ‣ A Convergence Analysis of Adaptive Optimizers under Floating-point Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A3.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.21314v1/muon_cifar10_grad.svg" id="A3.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:467/304;" width="467" height="304"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A3.F8.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="A3.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">CIFAR-10: Muon gradient norms under different mantissa precisions <math id="A3.F8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Larger mantissa bit-lengths lead to smaller converged gradient norms. Together with Figure <a href="#A3.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ C.3 Real-data Experiments ‣ Appendix C Additional Experiments and Details ‣ A Convergence Analysis of Adaptive Optimizers under Floating-point Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, this demonstrates that higher precision reduces quantization error and improves convergence, consistent with Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 4.6 (Convergence of Quantized Muon). ‣ 4.2 Theoretical results of Muon ‣ 4 Main Results ‣ A Convergence Analysis of Adaptive Optimizers under Floating-point Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A3.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.21314v1/adam_cifar10_qe.png" id="A3.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:467/264;" width="467" height="264" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A3.F9.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>: </span><span id="A3.F9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">CIFAR-10: Adam relative quantization error of different mantissa bits (<math id="A3.F9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>). Weights error (top left), Gradient error (top right), First moment error (bottom left), Second moment error (bottom right). These results show that the more mantissa bits, the smaller the relative quantization error. Combining with Figure <a href="#A3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ C.3 Real-data Experiments ‣ Appendix C Additional Experiments and Details ‣ A Convergence Analysis of Adaptive Optimizers under Floating-point Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, we can see that the more mantissa bits, the smaller quantization error, the better convergence performance (Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 4.5 (Convergence of Quantized Adam). ‣ 4.1 Theoretical results of Adam ‣ 4 Main Results ‣ A Convergence Analysis of Adaptive Optimizers under Floating-point Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A3.F10" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.21314v1/muon_cifar10_qe.png" id="A3.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:467/263;" width="467" height="263" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A3.F10.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 10</span>: </span><span id="A3.F10.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">CIFAR-10: Muon with auxiliary Adam relative quantization error of different mantissa bits (<math id="A3.F10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>). Weights error (top left), Gradient error (top middle), Momentum error (top right), Auxiliary Adam first moment error (bottom left), Auxiliary Adam second moment error (bottom middle). These results show that the more mantissa bits, the smaller the relative quantization error. Combining with Figure <a href="#A3.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ C.3 Real-data Experiments ‣ Appendix C Additional Experiments and Details ‣ A Convergence Analysis of Adaptive Optimizers under Floating-point Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, we can see that the more mantissa bits, the smaller quantization error, the better convergence performance (Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 4.6 (Convergence of Quantized Muon). ‣ 4.2 Theoretical results of Muon ‣ 4 Main Results ‣ A Convergence Analysis of Adaptive Optimizers under Floating-point Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
